#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125630} :activity/id #uuid "304b28d7-9caa-5437-822d-7b03dc2aef7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125816 :tutorial/content "So, given a stationary critical point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R},$ how do we classify it?\n\nOne way is to consider the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $\\mathbf{a}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ is a stationary point, we have $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nLet's assume that $\\mathbf x$ is close to $\\mathbf a,$ $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0,$ and that the terms of cubic order and higher are sufficiently small. Then, we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is an $n \\times n$ *symmetric* matrix. Thus, the term\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nis a *quadratic form*.\n\n\\[ \n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf a)\n=\n\\left.\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_1^2} & \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1\\partial x_2} & \\cdots & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_1 \\partial x_n} \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_2\\partial x_1 } & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_2^2} & \\cdots & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_2 \\partial x_n} \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_n\\partial x_1 } & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_n\\partial x_2} & \\cdots & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_n^2}\n\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\right|_{\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we note the following:\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, this quadratic form is always positive, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) > 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) > f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *minimum*.\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, this quadratic form is always negative, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) < 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) < f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *maximum.*\n\n- If this quadratic form is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point.\n\nThe assumption $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0$ is important. If $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) =0,$ then the quadratic form is zero along some line through $\\mathbf a$ or zero *everywhere* in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a.$ In such cases, we cannot ignore the higher-order terms and must use another method to classify the critical point.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider cases where $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0.$\n\nFor example, suppose we're told that [math]\\mathbf{a}[/math] is a critical point of the function [math]f[/math] and\n\n\\[\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) =\n\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThen, we can classify the critical point by classifying [math]\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})[/math] as the matrix of a quadratic form.\n\nCalculating the eigenvalues of the matrix, we find that one is positive and the other is negative:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\lambda_1 &= -2 < 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\\lambda_2 &= 3 > 0\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, [math]\\mathbf{a}[/math] is a saddle point of [math]f.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "3cedcd0c-77c7-5789-b76d-7ae3307c237b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4263 :tutorial/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Taylor Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "f3f90cb9-acd8-5630-859d-3bd37e502189" :step/math-academy-id 25915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125817 :tutorial/content "Recall that the *derivative* of a multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAnalogously with functions of one variable, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is a **critical point** of $f$ if $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ or does not exist.\n\n- Critical points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ are known as **stationary points.**\n\nThe local extrema of a function $f$ occur at the critical points. So, we can find the local extrema of $f$ by determining the stationary points *and* the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ does not exist and classify them accordingly (we'll discuss how to do this shortly). \n\nNot all of the solutions of $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ give rise to local extrema! Solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ that *do not* give rise to local extreme values are called **saddle points**.\n\nFor example, consider the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/cea092da684db6b055c78369fcb987c3577a027ef0d8f03d598e115e85775ff5.png)\n\nIt's easy to check that $\\boldsymbol f'(0,0) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and so $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f.$ This point is a saddle point. Note that the surface curves up in one direction (the red curve) and down in a different direction (the green curve), resembling a horse riding saddle.\n\nIn any neighborhood of a saddle point $\\mathbf a,$ there are points $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ such that $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'-\\mathbf{a}$ are orthogonal and they satisfy\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf a) >0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad f(\\mathbf{x}') - f(\\mathbf a) <0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60d15795-6c7a-5ec9-806d-625f5d8c5c85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4264 :tutorial/title "Saddle Points"} :step/id #uuid "42eecd67-0de9-5101-84f7-5ef560683d60" :step/math-academy-id 25917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124379 :question/id #uuid "c6938151-ab77-5288-ba08-156dc942ddec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7030"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"} :step/id #uuid "98e2abbe-ffee-53fc-af20-297624f9d77a" :step/math-academy-id 25918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124209 :question/id #uuid "137818ca-6c48-5ae1-8788-2015f7890ad5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12460"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401426}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "1bf38d19-ac54-50d2-9456-e7e1e26fc0a6" :step/math-academy-id 44845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124210 :question/id #uuid "976e4ec4-93f9-57a4-9f2b-2116e09fed22" :question/math-academy-id "e-12461"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401439}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "4b1acac2-a584-5026-9cb7-d5e4ae42f3ee" :step/math-academy-id 44846 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125873 :tutorial/content "Now, recall the definition of local extrema for single-variable functions:\n\n- A function $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\geq f(a)$ for all $x$-values in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- A function $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\leq f(a)$ for all $x$-values in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\nOn the function's graph, a local minimum is the lowest point of a valley, and a local maximum is the highest point of a peak.\n\n![](images/ba/ba5be082363bd963514baa6b1a777f02098d53a3b0a80ddc2fad231e4669ebd7.png)\n\nWe can generalize the concept of local extrema to multivariable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ as follows:\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a local minimum at the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x}$ in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a local maximum at the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x}$ in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nIn particular, for a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R},$ local minima and maxima are the lowest points in valleys and highest points in peaks, respectively, in the graph $z=f(x,y)$ of the function.\n\n![](images/74/74d04165fd3bd5cd24ae82351bbf840e7d549b272c3deea16671dbfcd40bc75a.png)\n\nFor the points $A$ and $B,$ we can take neighborhoods of the $(x,y)$-values at these points such that $A$ and $B$ are the lowest points on the graph in these neighborhoods. Hence, they are local minima.\n\nSimilarly, for the points $C$ and $D,$ we can take neighborhoods of the $(x,y)$-values at these points such that $C$ and $D$ are the highest points on the graph in these neighborhoods. Hence, they are local maxima.\n\n**Note**: For the point $E,$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_E,y_E) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ or $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$, the point $E$ satisfies the conditions for a local minimum *and* a local maximum.\n\nFinally, the points $F$ and $G$ are neither local minima nor local maxima. Notice that any neighborhood of $F$ will contain both points that lie higher than $F$ and lower than $F$ on the graph (similarly, for the point $G$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc6789c5-925e-54f4-9a5a-bfb179e77937" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7752 :tutorial/title "Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "086acac3-3967-5f92-bf6e-ae6524a36328" :step/math-academy-id 44870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124211 :question/id #uuid "94c54743-ba74-51c5-9c59-777125bb76f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12465"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401419}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ab662d13-5448-5cfa-8e5b-f6bf4d245b75" :step/math-academy-id 44871 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125874 :tutorial/content "Recall the following definitions for single-variable functions:\n\n- A function $f(x)$ over the domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a global minimum point at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\geq f(a)$ for all $x \\in D.$\n\n- A function $f(x)$ over the domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a global maximum point at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\leq f(a)$ for all $x \\in D.$\n\nOn the function's graph, the global minimum and maximum are the lowest and highest points, respectively.\n\n![](images/63/633526565293f480a6a5fc45b87a70d38e946ae0f72a925f0074776e201a43a4.png)\n\nWe can generalize this concept to multivariable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a **global minimum point** at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x} \\in D.$ The value $f(\\mathbf{a})$ is the **global minimum** of the function, the smallest value the function attains on its domain.\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a **global maximum point** at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x} \\in D.$ The value $f(\\mathbf{a})$ is the **global maximum** of the function, the largest value the function attains on its domain.\n\nIn particular, for a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R},$ the global minimum and maximum are the lowest and highest points on the graph $z=f(x,y),$ respectively.\n\n![](images/de/de4cfc96fdce210430291999e5997f4a0c39ed1fbe63c3848b61508ca3f04114.png)\n\nFor the function shown above:\n\n- The minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = -2,$ so the global minimum is $-2,$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y) = (1,-1).$ Therefore the global minimum point is $(1,-1,-2).$\n\n- Similarly, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y) = (1,1).$ Therefore the global maximum point is $(1,1,2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c71de7-b70c-5b7f-a566-143fd897ff59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7753 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d367e806-9c4c-5d98-bc43-97c56882eee8" :step/math-academy-id 44876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125885 :tutorial/content "To summarize, the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a stationary point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab23ca4b-1bff-5c96-a4d2-62b72b8adff2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7852 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Key Results"} :step/id #uuid "a55eef78-6bcb-5da4-be6c-3164a273066c" :step/math-academy-id 45322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125629}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124736}]}
